WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.350
Who doesn't love cookies?

00:00:01.350 --> 00:00:04.660
This is a recipe that's going
to be very straightforward.

00:00:04.660 --> 00:00:07.080
You're going to wow everybody
who has these cookies.

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:09.780
You are going to learn all of
the tricks of making the best

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:10.750
cookie of your life.

00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:12.670
Let's get started.

00:00:12.670 --> 00:00:15.900
Let's start with the really
basic, which is mise en place.

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:19.320
What that means is gathering
everything in its place

00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:20.670
before you start the recipe.

00:00:20.670 --> 00:00:22.260
So I start with the chocolate.

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:24.210
With a chef's knife,
I'm just going

00:00:24.210 --> 00:00:31.570
to take this chunk of chocolate
and cut down like this.

00:00:31.570 --> 00:00:34.290
So I'm going to make sure all
of the chocolate is about half

00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:35.850
an inch to an inch in size.

00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:37.672
For me, a chocolate
chunk is what

00:00:37.672 --> 00:00:39.630
I want when I'm eating
a chocolate chip cookie.

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:42.690
Place your bowl on the scale,
and then you press tare.

00:00:42.690 --> 00:00:45.420
And that zeros it out so that
it doesn't take into account

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:46.590
the weight of the bowl.

00:00:46.590 --> 00:00:54.480
I'm going to start measuring
till I get about 255.

00:00:54.480 --> 00:00:57.633
So I have 255
grams of chocolate.

00:00:57.633 --> 00:00:59.300
Can you imagine if I
tried to figure out

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:00.710
how many cups this is?

00:01:00.710 --> 00:01:03.410
Like if you chop your
chocolate really small

00:01:03.410 --> 00:01:05.175
and then if I chop
it kind of big,

00:01:05.175 --> 00:01:07.550
we're going to end up with
two totally different amounts.

00:01:07.550 --> 00:01:09.060
I weigh everything.

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:10.610
I'm going to tare again.

00:01:10.610 --> 00:01:12.050
So that zeros it out.

00:01:12.050 --> 00:01:14.833
Now I can add milk chocolate.

00:01:14.833 --> 00:01:16.500
I'm just going to
start shaving it down.

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:19.070
This one's a little bit easier
to cut because it's softer.

00:01:19.070 --> 00:01:23.600
I need about 70 grams,
so I've tared the scale.

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:25.680
72, almost perfect.

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:28.520
So now we have our 255
grams of dark chocolate

00:01:28.520 --> 00:01:30.860
and about 70 grams
of milk chocolate.

00:01:30.860 --> 00:01:32.150
Now I've got my second bowl.

00:01:32.150 --> 00:01:35.640
150 grams of granulated sugar.

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:38.960
This is three quarters of a cup.

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:43.970
I'm just going to measure this
out, and we've got our 150.

00:01:43.970 --> 00:01:46.070
And then in the
same bowl, I'm going

00:01:46.070 --> 00:01:47.660
to measure the Brown sugar.

00:01:47.660 --> 00:01:48.890
Tare that.

00:01:48.890 --> 00:01:51.650
Now brown sugar
is a great example

00:01:51.650 --> 00:01:56.120
of why we want to use a scale
rather than a measuring cup.

00:01:56.120 --> 00:01:59.120
It looks like it's taking up
this much room in the bowl.

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:01.110
But you can pack it down.

00:02:01.110 --> 00:02:04.430
So that means that if you're
measuring brown sugar using

00:02:04.430 --> 00:02:06.740
a cup measurement, if I'm
measuring brown sugar using

00:02:06.740 --> 00:02:08.449
a cup measurement,
we might end up

00:02:08.449 --> 00:02:10.160
with two totally
different measurements

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:13.100
depending on who packs
down the brown sugar more.

00:02:13.100 --> 00:02:15.050
So that's why we
measure with the scale.

00:02:15.050 --> 00:02:18.292
In the same bowl, 165
grams of brown sugar.

00:02:18.292 --> 00:02:20.000
The next thing we're
going to measure out

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:21.377
is our dry ingredients.

00:02:21.377 --> 00:02:23.210
I'm going to start out
with a nice big bowl,

00:02:23.210 --> 00:02:26.150
and remember to tare so
that you start back at 0.

00:02:26.150 --> 00:02:29.210
High gluten bread
flour, 160 grams.

00:02:29.210 --> 00:02:31.202
By measuring everything
out with a scale,

00:02:31.202 --> 00:02:32.910
it also just makes
things so much easier.

00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:34.880
You don't have to have
multiple cup measures.

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:36.710
You can just go ahead
and dump everything

00:02:36.710 --> 00:02:38.960
straight into the dry mix.

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:40.400
I actually like
a cookie that has

00:02:40.400 --> 00:02:43.460
a little bit of like thickness
and a little bit of chew.

00:02:43.460 --> 00:02:45.920
So we've added the
high gluten flour,

00:02:45.920 --> 00:02:47.330
going to tare that again.

00:02:47.330 --> 00:02:51.690
Now we have your basic all
purpose flour, 150 grams.

00:02:51.690 --> 00:02:54.950
So now we've got
the flour measured.

00:02:54.950 --> 00:03:00.300
We're going to add a
teaspoon of baking soda.

00:03:00.300 --> 00:03:03.380
Now with stuff like
baking soda, we're

00:03:03.380 --> 00:03:05.750
using these items,
these ingredients

00:03:05.750 --> 00:03:09.020
in such small measures that the
scale doesn't really register.

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:11.060
I go with the teaspoon measure.

00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:12.740
So we've got a teaspoon
of baking soda.

00:03:12.740 --> 00:03:17.670
We have a half a
teaspoon of kosher salt.

00:03:17.670 --> 00:03:19.250
So I have two large eggs.

00:03:19.250 --> 00:03:20.810
And these are at
room temperature.

00:03:20.810 --> 00:03:24.260
And you're going to crack them
directly into a small bowl.

00:03:24.260 --> 00:03:27.650
Then we're going to add a
teaspoon of vanilla extract.

00:03:27.650 --> 00:03:30.510
I don't want little pockets
of egg yolk to be in the mix.

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:32.515
So I'm going to
whisk it all together

00:03:32.515 --> 00:03:33.890
to try to create
something that's

00:03:33.890 --> 00:03:36.350
a little bit homogeneous.

00:03:36.350 --> 00:03:39.620
The eggs are at room
temperature because when

00:03:39.620 --> 00:03:42.410
we are going to mix the
cookie dough together,

00:03:42.410 --> 00:03:44.330
we're going to
spend a lot of time

00:03:44.330 --> 00:03:46.430
creaming that butter
and sugar together.

00:03:46.430 --> 00:03:48.290
The butter is going
to start to soften.

00:03:48.290 --> 00:03:50.300
It's going to get aerated
with all of the sugar.

00:03:50.300 --> 00:03:54.620
If the eggs are cold, as soon
as the eggs hit the butter

00:03:54.620 --> 00:03:57.508
and sugar, it's going to
cause the butter to firm up.

00:03:57.508 --> 00:04:00.050
We're going to cream the heck
out of it, make it really light

00:04:00.050 --> 00:04:00.770
and fluffy.

00:04:00.770 --> 00:04:02.360
Let's put together this dough.

00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:04.970
We're going to start
with unsalted butter.

00:04:04.970 --> 00:04:07.610
The ideal temperature when
creaming butter and sugar

00:04:07.610 --> 00:04:10.460
when you have a stick like
this is that you can bend it.

00:04:10.460 --> 00:04:15.080
First, mixing up the butter
and then adding the sugar.

00:04:15.080 --> 00:04:17.930
If you buy salted butter
in the grocery store,

00:04:17.930 --> 00:04:19.190
there are different brands.

00:04:19.190 --> 00:04:21.050
And you really don't
know how much salt

00:04:21.050 --> 00:04:22.520
has been added to the butter.

00:04:22.520 --> 00:04:25.100
Creaming butter and
sugar, that is not a step

00:04:25.100 --> 00:04:26.270
that you want to skip.

00:04:26.270 --> 00:04:29.400
It adds such a huge difference
to the texture of the cookie

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:32.060
so that you get that really
nice light chewy texture.

00:04:32.060 --> 00:04:34.790
If it gets to the point where
the butter is so warm that it

00:04:34.790 --> 00:04:38.090
starts to melt, all of those
air pockets start to collapse.

00:04:38.090 --> 00:04:40.340
After you've put the
butter in the mixing bowl

00:04:40.340 --> 00:04:42.210
is to then add the sugar.

00:04:42.210 --> 00:04:44.540
We've got both the
mix of the brown sugar

00:04:44.540 --> 00:04:45.660
and the white sugar.

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:47.370
We're going to go
ahead and get started.

00:04:47.370 --> 00:04:49.730
Let's start this
at a slow speed.

00:04:49.730 --> 00:04:52.220
It should take about 3
to 5, maybe 8 minutes

00:04:52.220 --> 00:04:55.880
to get this beautiful fluffy
butter and sugar mixture.

00:04:55.880 --> 00:04:58.760
Basically, the term creaming
butter and sugar together

00:04:58.760 --> 00:05:01.790
is when you mix butter and
sugar together and keep

00:05:01.790 --> 00:05:03.560
mixing it, and mixing
it, and mixing it

00:05:03.560 --> 00:05:05.640
until it lightens in color.

00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:07.610
In fact, it turns
to almost the color

00:05:07.610 --> 00:05:10.130
of like heavy cream, which is
how it gets its name, creaming

00:05:10.130 --> 00:05:11.420
butter and sugar together.

00:05:11.420 --> 00:05:13.313
Now why is that so important?

00:05:13.313 --> 00:05:15.230
If you were to have a
microscope here and take

00:05:15.230 --> 00:05:17.030
either the brown sugar
or the white sugar

00:05:17.030 --> 00:05:18.680
and look at it
under a microscope,

00:05:18.680 --> 00:05:21.020
you know that
sugar is a crystal.

00:05:21.020 --> 00:05:23.180
Now these hard
crystals when they

00:05:23.180 --> 00:05:29.130
go into your softened butter are
basically aerating your butter.

00:05:29.130 --> 00:05:33.690
I'm going to take a spatula
and really get into the bowl.

00:05:33.690 --> 00:05:36.660
A lot of times, the
butter will collect.

00:05:36.660 --> 00:05:40.230
Make sure that it gets loosened
so that it can all get creamed

00:05:40.230 --> 00:05:43.500
together with your sugar.

00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:46.170
Imagine all of those
sugar crystals creating

00:05:46.170 --> 00:05:48.750
all of the air in
the butter, and just

00:05:48.750 --> 00:05:50.310
let your mind
relax a little bit.

00:05:52.840 --> 00:05:55.180
Just in case there's
any last bits

00:05:55.180 --> 00:05:56.890
that I didn't get
the first time,

00:05:56.890 --> 00:05:58.660
I usually do this about twice.

00:05:58.660 --> 00:06:02.780
Now these eggs are again
at room temperature.

00:06:02.780 --> 00:06:06.670
So I'm going to turn the
mixer on kind of a low speed.

00:06:06.670 --> 00:06:10.240
Slowly drizzle it in so that
it has time to fully mix in

00:06:10.240 --> 00:06:12.420
to the butter and sugar.

00:06:12.420 --> 00:06:12.990
Great.

00:06:12.990 --> 00:06:17.590
Now the egg, and the butter,
and the sugar are all mixed in.

00:06:17.590 --> 00:06:19.890
So it's not going to be
quite as fluffy because we've

00:06:19.890 --> 00:06:21.030
added a little bit of egg.

00:06:21.030 --> 00:06:23.040
But it should be kind
of creamy looking.

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:26.427
And you don't want pieces of egg
mixed in with pieces of butter.

00:06:26.427 --> 00:06:28.260
You really want it to
be kind of homogeneous

00:06:28.260 --> 00:06:31.437
because if it's separated, then
when the dry ingredients hit

00:06:31.437 --> 00:06:34.020
it, then the dry ingredients are
going to latch on to the egg,

00:06:34.020 --> 00:06:35.410
and they're going to
latch on to the butter,

00:06:35.410 --> 00:06:37.618
and you're going to end up
with weird little pockets.

00:06:37.618 --> 00:06:40.260
Let me give it another scrape.

00:06:40.260 --> 00:06:42.990
Make sure we get all of that.

00:06:42.990 --> 00:06:45.090
And this is looking
really beautiful.

00:06:45.090 --> 00:06:46.830
Now I did want to
show you-- you can

00:06:46.830 --> 00:06:49.440
see like when I
cracked the egg, there

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:51.580
was a little bit of egg shell.

00:06:51.580 --> 00:06:54.660
So this is why we
crack into the bowl

00:06:54.660 --> 00:06:57.120
and then pour the eggs
because if this ends up

00:06:57.120 --> 00:06:58.620
in somebody's
cookie-- have you ever

00:06:58.620 --> 00:07:00.180
had a cookie with egg shell?

00:07:00.180 --> 00:07:02.250
It's like-- you'd think
it wouldn't be so bad.

00:07:02.250 --> 00:07:05.220
You think it'd be nice and
crunchy, but it's pretty nasty.

00:07:05.220 --> 00:07:05.760
Great.

00:07:05.760 --> 00:07:12.510
Let's now mix our chocolate
in with the flour.

00:07:12.510 --> 00:07:15.540
So the reason I mix the
chocolate into the flour

00:07:15.540 --> 00:07:20.160
is that that way I can combine
the flour into the egg, sugar,

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:22.800
and butter mixture with
minimal gluten development

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:24.360
because, remember,
the gluten will

00:07:24.360 --> 00:07:27.465
start developing as soon as
the flour hits anything liquid.

00:07:32.900 --> 00:07:37.190
I like to mix this
part with the mixer.

00:07:37.190 --> 00:07:39.650
And then I like to finish
it by hand because I

00:07:39.650 --> 00:07:40.970
don't want to over mix.

00:07:40.970 --> 00:07:43.950
If I were to turn this on, all
the flour would go everywhere.

00:07:43.950 --> 00:07:47.030
So I'm just going to
pulse on/off, on/off.

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:52.880
So I've mixed it pretty
much all the way.

00:07:52.880 --> 00:07:55.190
But I'm actually going to
finish mixing it by hand.

00:07:55.190 --> 00:07:56.660
I like to finally
feel the dough.

00:07:56.660 --> 00:07:58.250
I've let the machine
do everything.

00:07:58.250 --> 00:08:01.350
And now I want to do something.

00:08:01.350 --> 00:08:04.760
So let's go ahead
and take this out.

00:08:04.760 --> 00:08:09.530
And then also it allows me to
control how much mixing we're

00:08:09.530 --> 00:08:12.140
doing so that we don't
overwork the dough.

00:08:12.140 --> 00:08:15.240
Butter and sugar both
inhibit gluten development.

00:08:15.240 --> 00:08:18.350
So I'm not too worried about
getting a cookie dough that's

00:08:18.350 --> 00:08:19.320
going to be tough.

00:08:19.320 --> 00:08:22.790
But as I'm mixing, I make
sure it's all been mixed in,

00:08:22.790 --> 00:08:24.510
and now it's ready to go.

00:08:24.510 --> 00:08:26.570
Now this chocolate
chip cookie dough,

00:08:26.570 --> 00:08:28.700
you don't want to
bake it right now.

00:08:28.700 --> 00:08:30.470
These will be a
thousand times better

00:08:30.470 --> 00:08:33.320
if you let the dough rest
overnight in the fridge.

00:08:33.320 --> 00:08:36.590
That flour is going to absorb
more of the liquid that's

00:08:36.590 --> 00:08:38.360
in the eggs and in the butter.

00:08:38.360 --> 00:08:40.790
And it's going to bake off
into a much better cookie.

00:08:43.909 --> 00:08:48.410
So take your dough, and scrape
it into an airtight container.

00:08:48.410 --> 00:08:50.210
Take the time to rest the dough.

00:08:50.210 --> 00:08:52.580
What that does is it
hydrates the flour

00:08:52.580 --> 00:08:55.720
so that you end up with a really
beautiful, thick chocolate chip

00:08:55.720 --> 00:08:56.220
cookie.

00:08:56.220 --> 00:08:58.387
And now we're going to scoop
and bake these cookies.

00:08:58.387 --> 00:09:02.900
We're going to go ahead and put
about six cookies per trade.

00:09:02.900 --> 00:09:05.568
I like to place them
2 to 3 inches apart.

00:09:05.568 --> 00:09:07.110
They're going to
spread a little bit.

00:09:07.110 --> 00:09:09.380
So you do want to give
them a little bit of room.

00:09:09.380 --> 00:09:13.250
Ideally, you're going to want to
bake one cookie tray at a time

00:09:13.250 --> 00:09:15.710
because, that way,
you'll have even airflow

00:09:15.710 --> 00:09:17.360
above and below
the cookie sheet.

00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:19.730
Your cookies will bake
off nice and evenly.

00:09:19.730 --> 00:09:21.492
But let's be real.

00:09:21.492 --> 00:09:22.700
You've got two cookie sheets.

00:09:22.700 --> 00:09:23.870
Are you really going
to want to wait?

00:09:23.870 --> 00:09:26.005
So I won't be upset if
you bake two at a time.

00:09:26.005 --> 00:09:27.380
Just know that if
you really want

00:09:27.380 --> 00:09:30.540
to do this perfectly right,
you should bake one at a time.

00:09:30.540 --> 00:09:32.140
Now that I've got
my cookie scooped,

00:09:32.140 --> 00:09:33.890
we're going to go ahead
and go to the oven

00:09:33.890 --> 00:09:35.270
and start baking our cookies.

00:09:35.270 --> 00:09:36.853
Make sure you have
an oven thermometer

00:09:36.853 --> 00:09:39.560
so that you know that the
oven is actually at 350.

00:09:39.560 --> 00:09:41.870
So let's go ahead
and go to the oven.

00:09:41.870 --> 00:09:44.600
At the end of the day,
you are in your kitchen,

00:09:44.600 --> 00:09:47.540
looking at your product,
working with your oven.

00:09:47.540 --> 00:09:49.550
And so I really want
you to learn how

00:09:49.550 --> 00:09:51.890
to bake by sight and by touch.

00:09:51.890 --> 00:09:55.340
Even though you might have an
oven thermometer that says 350,

00:09:55.340 --> 00:09:57.710
my oven thermometer
says 350, there's

00:09:57.710 --> 00:09:59.420
so much variation in ovens.

00:09:59.420 --> 00:10:02.540
The oven isn't just
staying at a static 350.

00:10:02.540 --> 00:10:05.120
What happens is the
oven is triggered.

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:08.132
Once it goes below a certain
point, the heat kicks in again.

00:10:08.132 --> 00:10:09.840
And then it brings it
up a certain point.

00:10:09.840 --> 00:10:13.880
And so ovens kind of waver
between 10 degrees, 5 degrees.

00:10:13.880 --> 00:10:15.080
It depends on your oven.

00:10:15.080 --> 00:10:17.863
So I just pulled these
amazing chocolate chip cookies

00:10:17.863 --> 00:10:20.030
out of the oven, and I'm
going to let them rest here

00:10:20.030 --> 00:10:22.100
for about 10 minutes
before I take them off

00:10:22.100 --> 00:10:23.540
and put them on
the cooling tray.

00:10:23.540 --> 00:10:26.480
I think we should try
some of the warm cookies.

00:10:26.480 --> 00:10:27.808
Oh my gosh.

00:10:27.808 --> 00:10:28.850
Is your cookie like this?

00:10:28.850 --> 00:10:30.980
There's like gooey
milk chocolate

00:10:30.980 --> 00:10:33.840
and gooey dark chocolate inside.

00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:35.794
And so let's go ahead and--

00:10:35.794 --> 00:10:38.400
mm.

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:39.990
What am I looking for?

00:10:39.990 --> 00:10:43.620
You can see visually your
cookies are golden brown.

00:10:43.620 --> 00:10:45.510
It's pale in the center.

00:10:45.510 --> 00:10:48.450
But they're totally cooked
through in the center.

00:10:48.450 --> 00:10:50.550
If you pull them out
too early, you're

00:10:50.550 --> 00:10:53.555
going to end up with this
little pocket of raw dough.

00:10:53.555 --> 00:10:54.930
It won't be the
end of the world.

00:10:54.930 --> 00:10:56.190
But, ideally, you
want the cookie

00:10:56.190 --> 00:10:57.480
to be baked all the way through.

00:10:57.480 --> 00:10:59.188
For me, the perfect
chocolate chip cookie

00:10:59.188 --> 00:11:01.810
is kind of balanced with
a lot of chocolate flavor.

00:11:01.810 --> 00:11:03.510
It's got a crispy edge.

00:11:03.510 --> 00:11:06.390
It's got a tender melt
in your mouth center.

00:11:06.390 --> 00:11:09.730
It's got big pieces of
chocolate all throughout.

00:11:09.730 --> 00:11:12.810
There's also a little
hint of molasses.

00:11:12.810 --> 00:11:15.120
It's from the brown sugar
and a little caramel

00:11:15.120 --> 00:11:16.890
from the milk chocolate.

00:11:16.890 --> 00:11:19.120
And then there's just
a little bit of salt.

00:11:19.120 --> 00:11:21.460
We put in that kosher
salt into the recipe.

00:11:21.460 --> 00:11:23.850
And you can actually taste
a little hint of salt.

00:11:23.850 --> 00:11:25.650
It's not salty by any means.

00:11:25.650 --> 00:11:27.420
But it's enough salt
to really draw out

00:11:27.420 --> 00:11:29.970
the flavors of the butter,
the vanilla, and all

00:11:29.970 --> 00:11:31.380
of the chocolate.

00:11:31.380 --> 00:11:34.830
They are definitely best eaten
day of that you bake them.

00:11:34.830 --> 00:11:36.390
But if you store
them, they should

00:11:36.390 --> 00:11:39.460
last about two more days
in an airtight container.

00:11:39.460 --> 00:11:42.210
So now you've become
a star cookie baker.

00:11:42.210 --> 00:11:44.910
If you really want to
improve your cookie baking,

00:11:44.910 --> 00:11:46.530
there's one simple trick.

00:11:46.530 --> 00:11:48.325
Just keep making cookies.

00:11:48.325 --> 00:11:49.950
And who's going to
complain about that?

00:11:49.950 --> 00:11:51.780
Making cookies over
and over again,

00:11:51.780 --> 00:11:53.670
you will get so much
practice, and you

00:11:53.670 --> 00:11:56.340
will learn how every single
little adjustment you make

00:11:56.340 --> 00:11:58.050
changes the result
of the cookie.

00:11:58.050 --> 00:12:00.510
And you'll be able to
fine-tune your own technique so

00:12:00.510 --> 00:12:02.760
that you can be the
best cookie baker ever.

00:12:02.760 --> 00:12:06.110
[MUSIC PLAYING]

